<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      F11. `pos.till.open.other` — opening a cash drawer for SOMEBODY ELSE.

      ══ The gap ══

      `TillServiceImpl.openTill` derived the cashier from the caller's own subject and bound the
      session with `findByCashierIdAndStatus(callerId, OPEN)`. The drawer therefore belonged to
      whoever pressed the button, and there was no way to say who it was for. Measured live on
      2026-08-12 through the gateway: manager@terrace.local POSTed
      {"branchId":…, "openingFloatPaisa":500000, "cashierId":"<the cashier>"} and the field was
      silently dropped — the 409 that came back read "Cashier already has an open till session:
      fefd7187-…", which is the MANAGER's own id, not the cashier's. Walkthrough §0 recorded the
      user-visible half: the manager counted a Rs 5,000.00 float and the cashier's terminal still
      read "No active till".

      Every cashier therefore had to count their own float, and "the duty manager counts the float
      and hands over the drawer" — the way cash custody actually works in a restaurant — was not
      expressible in the product at all.

      ══ Why a NEW code, and not a widening of pos.till.open ══

      `pos.till.open` is held by CASHIER (041), and it must stay that way: a cashier opening their
      OWN drawer is normal work. But opening a drawer under another person's name is a different
      act with a different consequence — the resulting variance is signed for by the named cashier,
      not by whoever opened it — and a cashier must not be able to do it. Extending `pos.till.open`
      to cover both would hand every cashier that power in the same edit, which is precisely the
      "never widen a permission to make a screen work" failure.

      The split mirrors the one POS already makes between `pos.order.void.own` and the unqualified
      void: same verb, narrower object, separate code.

      ══ Who holds it ══

      OWNER, TENANT_ADMIN, MANAGER — the three roles that already hold `pos.till.reconcile.override`
      (041) and `pos.till.review` (046), i.e. the people the product already trusts with somebody
      else's drawer AFTER the shift. Being trusted to sign off a variance and not to hand over the
      float it will be measured against would be an odd place to draw the line.

      CASHIER and WAITER hold neither this nor anything implying it.

      ══ Why the grant is explicit ══

      Granted explicitly, never through a catalogue-wide SELECT from `permissions`. Every blanket
      grant in this changelog (041's OWNER/TENANT_ADMIN backfill included) is point-in-time and
      retro-grants nothing added afterwards, which is why 055, 082, 083, 085 and 088 all name their
      holders. A permission no role holds yields a clean, confident 403 for every user including
      OWNER — indistinguishable from a deliberate denial.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-090-pos-till-open-other" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>pos.till.open.other — open a till session for a NAMED cashier, held by the roles that already review tills</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('pos.till.open.other', 'pos', 'Open a till session on behalf of another cashier, counting the float into their drawer')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'pos.till.open.other'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.till.open.other'),
              ('MANAGER',      'pos.till.open.other')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'pos.till.open.other';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'pos.till.open.other';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Same RAISE posture as 056, 057, 082, 083, 085 and 088. A permission nobody holds is invisible
      at runtime: the "Open a drawer" panel simply never renders and the endpoint answers 403 for
      every user on every tenant, which reads as a deliberate denial rather than a broken migration.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-090-verify-pos-till-open-other" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if pos.till.open.other exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'pos.till.open.other';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'pos.till.open.other is defined but granted to no role. TillController gates GET /api/v1/pos/tills/cashiers on it and TillServiceImpl.openTill requires it to name a target cashier, so no manager on any tenant could open a drawer for a cashier - as a normal-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
